96. Synchronization: The Art of Minimalism
    Synchronized blocks are necessary evils. Overuse them, and your app crawls.

    Developers often use synchronized to prevent race conditions. However, using synchronized excessively blocks all threads and reduces parallel performance.

    Pro Tips:

    Replace synchronized with ReadWriteLock for read-heavy workloads.
    Use ConcurrentHashMap—it’s thread-safe and fast.
    Code Win:

    private final ReadWriteLock lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();

    public void writeData(String data) {
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            // Write operation
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }
    Ultimate Performance Hack: Use ConcurrentHashMap
    Instead of manually handling locks, use ConcurrentHashMap—it’s already optimized for multi-threading!

    public class DataStore {
        private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> data = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
        public String getData(String key) {
            return data.get(key);  // Thread-safe read 🚀
        }
        public void updateData(String key, String value) {
            data.put(key, value);  // Thread-safe write 🔥
        }
    }
    ✅ Why is ConcurrentHashMap the best?

    Reads and writes don’t block each other (internally optimized).
    Faster than explicit locking mechanisms.
    Best for high-concurrency environments (like web applications, caching, and microservices).

